"""Job Search v2 taxonomy - the single source of truth for skills, roles,
industries, metros and recognised phrases used by the "Proof Match" engine.
 
The browser engine (kaidostar-frontend/job-engine.js) carries an identical copy
of this data, generated by scripts/gen_job_taxonomy_js.py. Never edit the JS copy
by hand: edit this file, re-run the generator, and the parity test confirms both
sides are byte-for-byte the same data.
 
Precision over recall. Every alias here is matched as a whole word (no letter or
digit on either side), so "java" never matches "javascript" and "excel" never
matches "excellent". Ambiguous everyday words (R, Go, Swift, Rust, Excel used as
a verb...) only count when written the way the skill is written AND, where
noted, when a context word sits in the same sentence.
 
Skill fields:
  id      stable key
  name    display name
  family  skills in the same family are ADJACENT (partial credit: Tableau vs
          Power BI, MySQL vs PostgreSQL). None = no adjacency.
  kind    hard | tool | soft | cert | lang. Soft skills never count toward the
          skills score - every posting asks for "communication" and every
          resume claims it, so counting it only inflates scores.
  aliases lower-case phrases matched case-insensitively
  cs      case-sensitive spellings matched against the original text
  ctx     for cs spellings: at least one of these words must appear in the
          same sentence (keeps "R" and "Go" from matching ordinary prose)
  implies having this skill means having these too (pandas -> python)
"""
 
